      "text": "(1) Let Jane's age at the present be $x$, and one year ago she was $(x-1)$ years old. Then,\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l}1 \\\\ x-1=y^2\\\\ +1 \\\\ x+1=z^3\\end{array}\\right.$, where $y$ and $z$ are integers and $z > 1$.\n\nDecrementing the congruence equations by 1 year from both side, we get:\n\n$\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l} 1-1 \\\\ x-1=y^2-1\\\\ +1 \\\\ x+1=z^3-1\\end{array}\\right.$, where $y$ and $z$ are integers and $z > 1$.\n\nThat is, $\\left\\{\\begin{array}\\ 0 \\\\ x-2=y^2\\\\ +2 \\\\ x+2=z^3-1\\end{array}\\right.$ where $y$ and $z$ are integers and $z > 1$\n\nSince we have $\\left\\{\\begin{array}{l} 0 \\\\ x-2=y^2\\\\ +2 \\\\ x+2=z^3-1\\end{array}\\right.$ where $y$ and $z$ are integers and $z > 1$,\n\nthen $x^2-2 < {(z-2)}^2 \\leq {{z}^3-3z+5}={{(z-1)}^3}+3z+4{{{(z-1)}^2}+6z+4}.$ Therefore, ${{(z-1)}^3}+3z+4 < {{z}^3-1}$.\n\nTheir difference is a perfect cube $= {{(z-1)}^3}-3z-4 = {{(z-1)}^3}-1$ can also be proven. If ${{(z-1)}^3}-1<y^2$, then, clearly, we have $1<y$ which is a contradiction since $2 < y^2 < z^3-1 < {{(z-1)}^3}+3z+4$.\n\nEventually, we have ${{(z-1)}^3}+3z+4=y^2$.\n\nRepresenting equation ${{(z-1)}^3}+3z+4=y^2$ as a difference of squares gives us\n\n$({{(z-1)}^2}+z+2)({{z}^2}-{z}+1)={{(z-2)}^2}({{(z-1)}^2}+1)$.\n\nSince $z$ is an integer greater than 1, the left-hand side or the right-hand side is always a perfect square, requiring both sides to be equal.\n\n$(z-1)^2+z+2 = z^2-z+1 $\n\nLet $w = z-1$ and the equation becomes ${{w}^2} + \\left(w+3\\right) = \\left(\\left(w+1\\right)^2 - {{w}^2}\\right) + \\left(4w+1\\right)= \\left(w-1\\right)^2 + \\left(4w+1\\right) = {4w+{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 4}}^{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 2} = {\\left(4w+{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 2}\\right)}^{\\cdot{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 3}} = (\\color{#FF0000}{2w+1})^{\\cdot{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 3}} = (2*w + 1)^{3*} = {w^{\\cdot{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 3}} + {w^{\\cdot{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 2}}} + w} = {(z-1)^{\\cdot{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 3}} + {(z-1)^{\\cdot{\\color{#FF0000}\\bf 2}}} + \\left(z-1\\right)}$ \n\nIf $w-1$ is even, then $4w+2 = 4w+1+\\left(4w+1\\right) > \\left(z-1\\right)^3$, which contradicts the original equation $5=z^3-3z-4<{{z^3-3z+5}}={{\\left(z-1\\right)}^3}+3z+4={{z^3}}$ < $\\left(z-1\\right)^3+\\left(4w+2\\right)<\\left(z-1\\right)^3+\\left(z+3\\right)={\\left(z-1\\right)^2}+z+2<<{z^2}$\n\nTherefore, $w = z-1$, $14-2$ years ago is Jane's age. Therefore, Jane is $16$ years old. Answer: $\\boxed{16}$.",
